The night train hissed through the black countryside, its headlamp cutting a thin line across the fields. Conductor Elias stood on the platform of the last car, checking tickets with a practiced flick of his pen. When the whistle blew, he turned to the rear and froze. A dark silhouette loomed where only empty track should have been—a carriage, sleek and unmarked, its windows glowing faintly amber. It hung there, coupler humming, as if it had always been part of the train. Elias frowned, his breath fogging in the cold air, and reached for the brass handle, wondering why the railway had slipped this extra weight into his night.

